<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#darb-el-arbain">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darb el-Arba'in</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Darb El Arba'in</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Darb el-Arba'in, the 'forty-day road', was a caravan route linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. It was the commercial lifeline of the Darfur Sultanate, which developed and controlled it, and it carried slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and other goods northwards as well as pilgrims bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. Control of the route was a central source of the sultans' wealth and regional importance.</p></div>
